"""Session 3. Measuring a model: accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC and AUC, thresholds, baselines, overfitting. A technical session (v3, 6 November 2026; terms and examples revised 6 November 2026; rebuilt 11 November 2026 so that every measure is explained before it is used).

Teaching order (11 November 2026): a smoke alarm (invented illustration numbers: 1,000 kitchen events, 20 real fires) carries the whole ladder, one measure per slide:
four outcomes as a story, then the names (confusion matrix, TP, FP, FN, TN), accuracy, the majority-class baseline, precision, recall (true positive rate),
why a plain average misleads, F1 as the harmonic mean, the score and the threshold, three thresholds with real counts, the false positive rate,
the ROC curve built point by point, AUC as the chance that a fire outscores a quiet event, and a one-page summary.
Then the late-invoice data are the application (matrix, baselines, six thresholds, ROC, cost built line by line, demonstration 1),
then overfitting recalled from Session 2 (the real depth curve with two checks, demonstration 2), then the hands-on work and the decision.
Every invoice number on the slides comes from the recorded run in demo_runs/s03/ (s03_measure.py, results.json) and demo_runs/s03_measuring_a_model.md:
a fictional Nile Valley Foods late-payment dataset of 3,000 invoices, fixed seeds, scikit-learn 1.9.1, 900 held-back test invoices of which 83 were paid late.
The assumed costs (UGX 20,000 to chase a flag, UGX 300,000 for a missed late invoice) are an assumption for the lesson, and the slides say so.
The smoke alarm numbers: 1,000 events, 20 fires, alarm rang 50 times: TP 16, FP 34, FN 4, TN 946. Precision 32%, recall 80%, accuracy 96.2%, F1 46%, FPR 3.5%. Never ringing: accuracy 98%, recall 0%.
Other alarm settings: high threshold rang 20 times (TP 12, FP 8, FN 8: precision 60%, recall 60%, FPR 0.82%); low threshold rang 200 times (TP 19, FP 181, FN 1: precision 9.5%, recall 95%, FPR 18%).
Cautious alarm (F1 example): TP 2, FP 0, FN 18: precision 100%, recall 10%, plain average 55%, F1 18%.
AUC example: fires score 8 and 6, quiet events 7, 3 and 2: 5 of 6 pairs ranked right, AUC 0.83 (checked with scikit-learn roc_auc_score).
Overfitting (coherence review, 11 November 2026): the separate illustration slide was removed, because Session 2 teaches overfitting; the depth-curve slide recalls Session 2 and adds the training and test gap and the baseline as checks.
"""

