    facilitator=dict(
        prep=['Python environment with scikit-learn: from the Build folder run `python demo_runs/s03/s03_threshold.py 0.50 0.20 0.05` once before class and check that the first row shows 9 flagged and 6 caught. Takes about 5 seconds.',
              'Open `demo_runs/s03/s03_measure.py` and `demo_runs/s03_measuring_a_model.md` in a second window as the fallback: every invoice number on the slides is printed there.',
              'Print the worksheet (in the worksheet): the twenty cases (slide with the list) and a blank 2 by 2 confusion matrix labelled true positive, false positive, false negative, true negative. Bring pencils and a calculator or the phone calculator. The exercise rows and answers are unchanged from the earlier version.',
              'NEW warm-up exercise for the worksheet (optional, on the page before the twenty cases): the smoke alarm matrix with 1,000 events: TP 16, FP 34, FN 4, TN 946. Questions: accuracy (962 ÷ 1,000 = 96.2%), precision (16 ÷ 50 = 32%), recall (16 ÷ 20 = 80%), F1 (2 × 0.32 × 0.80 ÷ 1.12 = 46%), and the accuracy of an alarm that never rings (980 ÷ 1,000 = 98%, recall 0%). These smoke alarm numbers are invented for the lesson; say so. The slides (11 November 2026) also use: high threshold TP 12, FP 8, FN 8 (precision 60%, recall 60%, FPR 8 ÷ 980 = 0.82%); low threshold TP 19, FP 181, FN 1 (precision 9.5%, recall 95%, FPR 181 ÷ 980 = 18%); false positive rate of our alarm 34 ÷ 980 = 3.5%; cautious alarm TP 2, FP 0, FN 18 (precision 100%, recall 10%, plain average 55%, F1 = 1 ÷ ((1 + 10) ÷ 2) = 0.18); AUC pairs example: fires score 8 and 6, quiet events 7, 3 and 2, 5 of 6 pairs right, AUC 0.83.',
              'The cost slide uses two ASSUMED figures (UGX 20,000 to chase a flag, UGX 300,000 for a false negative). Say they are assumptions, ask the learner for figures from their own work, and rerun with --chase and --miss.',
              'The data are invented for the lesson (Nile Valley Foods, fictional). Say so at the first demonstration.',
              'ANSWER KEY for the twenty cases, flag at 0.20 or more: TP 3 (cases 2, 16, 19), FP 2 (cases 1, 20), FN 3 (cases 7, 10, 15), TN 12. Precision 3 ÷ 5 = 60%, recall 3 ÷ 6 = 50%. At 0.50: flagged cases 16 and 19, both late: precision 100%, recall 2 ÷ 6 = 33%. At 0.15: flagged 1, 2, 7, 16, 19, 20, TP 4, FP 2: precision 4 ÷ 6 = 67%, recall 67%.'],
        demo_steps=['LIVE. Open a terminal in the Build folder. Activate the course environment.',
                    '1. Run `python demo_runs/s03/s03_threshold.py 0.50 0.30 0.20 0.15 0.10 0.05`. Read the first line aloud: 900 test invoices, 83 late, majority-class baseline 90.8%. Then read the 0.50, 0.20 and 0.05 rows against the slide table.',
                    '2. Run it again with `--miss 100000` and then `--miss 600000`. Show that the cheapest row moves. Ask the learner which cost figures are realistic for their business.',
                    '3. LIVE. Run `python demo_runs/s03/s03_measure.py`. Scroll to `always_no` (the majority-class baseline: accuracy 0.908, recall 0), `rule`, `deep_tree` (train_acc 1.0, test_acc 0.852) and `small_tree`.',
                    '4. Optional, LIVE: type any threshold the learner suggests into the script.',
                    'RECORDED: the slide tables and charts are drawn from `demo_runs/s03/results.json`, made on 6 November 2026 with scikit-learn 1.9.1. The numbers are the same on every run because the seeds are fixed. The smoke alarm and spam filter figures are invented illustrations, not runs.'],
        fallback='If the terminal fails, show the threshold table slide and read the rows. Then open `demo_runs/s03_measuring_a_model.md` on screen: it holds the full output of both scripts. The two demonstrations work as a slide walk-through.',
        misconceptions=[('A model that is 95 percent accurate is a good model', 'Accuracy depends on how common the thing is. If 91 of 100 invoices are on time, always predicting on time scores 91. Ask for the baseline, precision and recall.'),
                        ('A higher threshold is always better because the model is surer', 'A higher threshold makes the model more careful about what it flags, and it misses more. It trades one error for the other.'),
                        ('AUC tells me the model is good enough to use', 'AUC says how well the model ranks cases over every threshold. You still choose a threshold, and the costs of mistakes decide whether the result is good enough.'),
                        ('A perfect score on the training data is a sign of quality', 'It is a warning of overfitting. A model can memorise its training data, noise included. Judge it on a test set it never saw.')],
        questions=[('What is a baseline?', 'A simple reference model that the model must beat, such as the majority-class baseline (always predict the most common class), or a one-line rule.'),
                   ('What is the difference between precision and recall?', 'Precision: of the cases flagged, how many were real. Recall: of the real cases, how many were flagged.'),
                   ('Why can accuracy mislead?', 'When the thing you look for is rare, a model that never flags it still scores very high.'),
                   ('What does the threshold do?', 'It is the score at or above which the model says positive. Raising it gives fewer flags and more false negatives. Lowering it gives more flags and more false positives.')],
        rescue=['If the maths feels heavy, stay on the confusion matrix and two divisions: TP ÷ (TP + FP) and TP ÷ (TP + FN). Skip the F1 formula and the ROC curve, and come back to them in the handbook.',
                'If the room is quiet on the decision slide, give the late-payment example and ask which row of the cost slide the learner would pick.',
                'If time is short, drop the ROC slide and the domains table. Keep the baseline, the threshold table and the practice slide.',
                'If the exercise runs long, finish the first practice and set the second one as optional work at home.'],
        probes=[('Which is better, precision or recall?', 'Neither. It depends on the cost of each mistake. When a false negative is costly, such as a fraud alert, favour recall. When a false positive is costly, such as freezing a good customer account, favour precision.'),
                ('What is a good AUC?', 'My understanding is that 0.5 is a coin flip and 1.0 is perfect, and what counts as good depends on the task and on what a simple baseline gets. Ask for the baseline beside it.'),
                ('How do I know the vendor did not test on the training data?', 'You cannot see it from the number. Ask in writing which data it was measured on and whether the model had seen it, and ask for a test on your own cases.'),
                ('Does a bigger test set matter?', 'Yes. Our test set has only 83 late invoices, so one or two cases move a percentage visibly. A small test set gives a shaky figure. Ask how many real cases were in the test.'),
                ('Do language models like ChatGPT have a threshold and precision?', 'The yes or no tasks built on them do, and you can measure precision and recall on a test set of your own. Free-form answers need other checks, which Session 10 covers.'),
                ('Why not always lower the threshold until nothing is missed?', 'Because every extra flag costs staff time and goodwill. In our run the 0.05 threshold caught 78 of 83 late invoices but raised 448 false positives. The cost table shows where the balance sits for a given set of costs.')],
        takeaway=['VENDOR QUESTION SHEET: THE NUMBERS BEHIND AN AI CLAIM',
                  'Words: confusion matrix (TP, FP, FN, TN). Precision = TP ÷ (TP + FP). Recall (true positive rate) = TP ÷ (TP + FN). Accuracy = (TP + TN) ÷ (TP + TN + FP + FN). False positive rate = FP ÷ (FP + TN). F1 = 2 × precision × recall ÷ (precision + recall), the harmonic mean.',
                  'ROC curve: true positive rate plotted against false positive rate for every threshold. AUC = the area under it (0.5 coin flip, 1 perfect). Cost of errors = (TP + FP) × cost of chasing a flag + FN × cost of a miss. Smoke alarm check: accuracy 96.2%, precision 32%, recall 80%, false positive rate 3.5%, F1 46%.',
                  '1. What test data was the figure measured on, and had the model seen that data?',
                  '2. How common is the thing we look for? What does the majority-class baseline score?',
                  '3. What are precision and recall, and at which threshold?',
                  '4. Who chose the threshold, and what did each kind of mistake cost?',
                  '5. How does it compare with a simple rule or with our current process?',
                  '6. How many real cases were in the test? Few cases means a shaky figure.',
                  '7. Can we test it on 50 of our own cases before we sign?',
                  '8. How will you check it after launch, as our data changes?',
                  '9. Who reads the false positives, and how long does it take them?',
                  '10. What happens to a case the model gets wrong? Who can overrule it?',
                  'Remember: accuracy rewards doing nothing when the thing is rare. A perfect score on training data can mean overfitting.']),
